{"name":"Princess Bride","hex":"#f4c1c1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"princess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female member of a royal family other than a queen, especially a daughter or granddaughter of a monarch. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A woman or girl who excels in a given field or class."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/princess"},{"term":"bride","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A woman in the context of her own wedding; one who is going to marry or has just been married.","An object ardently loved."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["to make a bride of"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bride"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Princess Bride","blocks":[{"text":"The Princess Bride is a 1987 American fantasy-adventure-comedy film directed and co-produced by Rob Reiner and starring Cary Elwes, Robin Wright, Mandy Patinkin, André the Giant, Chris Sarandon, Christopher Guest, Wallace Shawn, Peter Falk, Fred Savage, Billy Crystal and Carol Kane. Adapted by William Goldman from his novel of the same name, it tells the story of a swashbuckling farmhand named Westley, accompanied by companions befriended along the way, who must rescue his kidnapped true love Princess Buttercup from the evil Prince Humperdinck. The film preserves the novel's metafictional narrative style by presenting the story as a book being read by a grandfather to his sick grandson."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/db/Princess_bride.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Princess_Bride","wikidata":"Q506418"},{"title":"Princess","blocks":[{"text":"Princess is a title used by a female member of a regnant monarch's family or by a female ruler of a principality. The male equivalent is a prince. Most often, the term has been used for the consort of a prince, or for the daughter of a monarch. A crown princess can be the heir apparent to the throne or the spouse of the heir apparent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Sigrid_Eriksdotter_Vasa.jpg/330px-Sigrid_Eriksdotter_Vasa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess","wikidata":"Q863048"},{"title":"Bride","blocks":[{"text":"A bride is a woman who is about to be married or who is a newlywed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Wed-dress-001.jpg/330px-Wed-dress-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bride","wikidata":"Q353673"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Fantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_and_Magic%2C_EMP%2C_Seattle_-_The_Princess_Bride_%2815197707344%29.jpg/500px-Fantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_and_Magic%2C_EMP%2C_Seattle_-_The_Princess_Bride_%2815197707344%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Fantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_and_Magic%2C_EMP%2C_Seattle_-_The_Princess_Bride_%2815197707344%29.jpg/1280px-Fantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_and_Magic%2C_EMP%2C_Seattle_-_The_Princess_Bride_%2815197707344%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Fantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_and_Magic%2C_EMP%2C_Seattle_-_The_Princess_Bride_%2815197707344%29.jpg/500px-Fantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_and_Magic%2C_EMP%2C_Seattle_-_The_Princess_Bride_%2815197707344%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Inigo Montoya's sword (left) crossed with the sword used by Count Rugen (right) in the duel at the castle; beside it, Count Rugen's six-fingered glove. At bottom is the prop sword used in Highlander: The Series.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFantasy_Worlds_of_Myth_a